WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.120
Aggressively unscripted video incoming,

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.860
so I am in Los Angeles for reasons that will become clear shortly

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:10.680
and I'm thinking right now about storytelling. So I have often said over the years

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.120
that I believe books belong to their readers.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:17.200
Like I don't think it matters whether an author intended a symbolic resonance in their story

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.900
as long as it's there because we're not reading the author, we're reading the book.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.660
Also, I don't think we should privilege the author's voice when it comes to matters outside the text

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.720
like, for instance, what happens to characters after the story ends

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.180
because I don't think that's up to the author, I think that's up to us as readers.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:37.020
My books belong to me while I am working on them and then when I am finished, they don't.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.980
Except... there are a couple problems with this worldview

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.320
One: It's a little bit disingenuous to pretend that the author isn't present in the book at all,

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:50.060
especially in our personality-driven culture and especially if the author has chosen to have a public life

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:53.440
by, for instance, participating in an 11 year long video blog conversation

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.840
And then, secondly, there's the problem that while the book belongs to the reader,

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.800
the author has to make a lot of decisions on behalf of the book

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.940
and that is true even after the book is published - which brings us to Los Angeles

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:11.480
So, I almost didn't sell the movie rights to The Fault In Our Stars, partly because I'd had some bad experiences in Hollywood

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.120
but partly because that book was really close to me.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:18.780
A big part about what I enjoy about writing made-up stories was that they're a kind of coded autobiography

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:22.240
in which only I have the complete key to the code

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.760
and TFiOS not only contained parts of me that lots of people knew about like my friendship with Esther Earl

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:30.940
but also parts of certain traumatic experiences I'd never told anyone about.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:33.540
I mean, obviously, it was exciting to imagine a movie of my book

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:39.520
but it was also scary to imagine some of that coded stuff being on a big screen for lots of people to watch.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.840
Also, SO MUCH CAN GO WRONG, I mean, they could have changed the whole plot,

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.220
they could have made Hazel an 84 year old survivor of the Titanic!

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:52.160
That's actually a pretty good idea for a movie of the story of the Titanic but it's around this frame of an 84 year old lady

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.280
This is not an original idea...

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.620
Anyway, in the end, I sold the movie rights and I got so lucky.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:02.680
I had no way of knowing that Elisabeth and Erin at Fox 2000 would be the perfect stewards for that movie

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.260
or that we would get amazing producers or the best possible screenwriters

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:09.660
or an amazing cast and end up with a movie I really loved.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.720
And then we got to do it again with Paper Towns.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.600
And then I thought "that was a good run" because Paper Towns didn't make as much money

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:21.820
and also because I'd decided to write this extremely quiet novel that takes place almost entirely inside of one person's head

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:24.640
which isn't the sort of thing that usually lends itself it film.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.960
The big challenge for me in Turtles All The Way Down was whether I could use language

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:32.080
to find some sort of direct form or expression for obsessive thoughts

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.200
rather than only relying on metaphor.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:40.340
I wanted to give readers not only a sense of what OCD is like, but, maybe at least a glimpse into what it is

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.040
which is, like, a profoundly non-visual thing.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:46.260
But as Issac, who produced The Fault In Our Stars and Paper Towns pointed out to me

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.560
obsessive thoughts are also a non-linguistic experience

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.640
and that didn't stop me from writing the book.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:53.480
So, for the last two months we've been talking about it

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.480
Is there a way to tell this story visually without relying on the old tropes

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.300
that are usually associated with portrayals of OCD?

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.880
And today, I get to announce that working with the same studio and production company that made

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:06.660
The Fault in Our Stars and Paper Towns, we're gonna give it a try.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.040
That doesn't mean there will definitely be a movie but it means that there might be one

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.620
so now's the time to begin inundating me with casting suggestions

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.140
because no one - ever - reads my Twitter bio.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:21.200
I am really excited for the opportunity and also the challenge of the Turtles All The Way Down movie

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:22.500
and I hope you're excited too.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.760
The book does belong to you but I had to make this decision on behalf of it

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.380
I really trust the people who are working on this.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.860
Thanks to everyone who has made this book a No. 1 New York Times Bestseller

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:32.540
for now 7 weeks straight.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:36.540
And if you care about the book please know that I will try to listen to you throughout this process

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.260
Hank, DFTBA, I will see you on Friday.

